Z||000003||Spektrum-87GT||09-05-2001||04:50 PM||||im having the same problem with mine, rpms ~1200-1300 and the oil pressure fluctuates, also, the water temp is not working. keep me posted if it changing the sensor works... ||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||09-05-2001||05:12 PM||||The coolant sensor is what tells the ECM what temperature of the engine is.  I think that's the one you are referring to.  On the V6 (2.8), it's screwed into the right hand side ("front" of engine - waterpump end) of the lower intake manifold, right in the center.  Just beneath the water outlet.  It has a 2-pin connector plugged into it.<P>I think you use a 3/4" wrench to get it out.  It's underneath the EGR vacuum solenoid, and there's lots of wire bundles on that end of the engine, so it's kind of tough to get to.    <P>By the way, this is <B>not</B> the same sensor that operates the temperature gage on the instrument panel in the car.<P>And, Welcome to the Forum    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   <BR>---------------------------------------<P>Notice how it takes Stu 2 paragraphs, to say what this next guy says in one sentence??   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><BR>...and we did post at the same time! <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 09-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||ka4nkf||09-05-2001||05:12 PM||||the sensor for the ecm is just above the water pump. (horizontal position)||||reg||1
Z||000006||ka4nkf||09-05-2001||05:15 PM||||Hey Stugood I believe we posted at the same time.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Voytek||09-05-2001||06:31 PM||||Somehow I doubt that this is your problem.<P>If you want to change the sensors (coolant temp sensor - turns on the fan, coolant temp sending unit - controls gauge on gauge panel), they are relatively inexpensive but really don't believe you need to do that (I think the sensors' locations posted here are correct - the sensors are brass, screwed directly into the block).<P>You may have one of few problems with your idle.  I would suggest two things for starters: run the computer tester (plug under the center console - you can buy a tester at most parts stores).  Then see if there are any codes stored in the computer (the check engine light will flash - code 12 is normal, it always starts out with this code and flashes 3 times).  Or take it to a shop and have the info retrieved.<P>2: I would check for any vacuum leaks, such as the EGR tube (from EGR valve to upper plenum).  I've had a problem with high RPM and turned out the tube was cracked at the plenum.  One way to check it is to remove it and look for a crack.  Another way is to start the car and spray a tiny bit of carburetor cleaner at the tube, right by the plenum (just a bit and using the little straw - this stuff is flammable). If the car stumbles, it means you have a leak there.  Replace the tube.<P>If no codes are stored, and the tube is OK, you may have a vacuum leak somewhere else - visual inspection is good.<P>Other than that, if all is OK, it is possible to set the correct RPM into the computer.  Most shops can do that with a laptop.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
